'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Salchichón and fuet belong to the same broad family of dry-cured sausages, so confusion between them is understandable. Both are commonly made from minced pork and fat, salt and seasonings, stuffed into a casing and fermented and dried. However, <strong>fuet is not simply another word for salchichón</strong>. Typical differences involve diameter, format, curing speed, texture and regional tradition.</p>
<h2>The most visible difference: diameter</h2>
<p>Fuet is usually narrow and elongated. Its smaller diameter allows moisture to leave relatively quickly and the sausage can develop a firm texture in a shorter period. Salchichón appears in a much wider range of sizes, including large cular formats.</p>
<p>Diameter strongly influences curing: a thick sausage needs slower, controlled drying so the centre and exterior develop evenly.</p>
<h2>Where does fuet come from?</h2>
<p>Fuet is especially associated with Catalan charcuterie. Recipes differ between producers, so there is no single formula that describes every fuet. Its slim format, sliced service and relatively gentle seasoning are among its most recognisable traits.</p>
<h2>What is salchichón?</h2>
<p>Salchichón is a broad Spanish cured-sausage category made in many regions and sizes. <strong>Iberian salchichón</strong> uses Iberian pork according to the corresponding description, which can give the fat and flavour a distinctive profile.</p>
<p>See our guide to <a href="/en/iberian-loin-chorizo-salchichon-differences-how-to-choose/">Iberian loin, chorizo and salchichón</a>.</p>
<h2>Seasoning</h2>
<p>Salchichón is commonly associated with black pepper and other spices without paprika dominating the mixture. That separates it from chorizo, whose traditional character depends strongly on paprika.</p>
<p>Fuet is also usually gently seasoned, although recipes may include pepper, garlic or other spices.</p>
<h2>White mould on the outside</h2>
<p>Many fuets develop a white surface flora as part of controlled maturation. On products designed for this process it can be completely normal and contribute to aroma and surface stability.</p>
<p>That does not mean every mould on every sausage is safe. Read our guide to <a href="/en/white-mold-on-chorizo-salchichon-when-normal-when-not/">white mould on cured sausages</a>.</p>
<h2>Texture</h2>
<p>Because fuet is narrow, it can become firm quite quickly. A thicker salchichón can retain more moisture in the centre and feel softer or more unctuous. Final texture also depends on curing time, fat ratio, mincing and drying conditions.</p>
<h2>Can you eat the casing?</h2>
<p>It depends on the casing. Natural casings may be edible; collagen and other manufactured casings vary. Check the label or producer instructions rather than assuming.</p>
<h2>Which is better for a charcuterie board?</h2>
<p>Fuet is easy to slice and approachable, while a good Iberian salchichón can offer greater richness and a more premium charcuterie character. They can also be served together to create contrast.</p>
<h2>How fermentation and drying shape the flavour</h2>
<p>Both products develop flavour through a combination of fermentation, moisture loss and maturation. The balance is influenced by recipe, starter cultures or naturally occurring microorganisms, temperature, humidity and time. A narrow sausage changes more quickly because it has more surface area in relation to its volume, while a larger salchichón can mature more gradually through the centre. This helps explain why two sausages made from broadly similar ingredients can taste surprisingly different.</p>
<p>Drying also concentrates salt, fat and aromatic compounds. A very dry fuet may therefore taste more intense than a softer example even if its seasoning was initially milder.</p>
<h2>How to choose between them in a shop</h2>
<p>Think first about how you plan to serve the product. Fuet is convenient for informal snacking, sandwiches and boards where a small easy-to-slice piece is useful. Salchichón offers a wider choice of diameters and is particularly interesting when you want thin slices from a larger cured piece. For Iberian salchichón, the quality and character of the pork and fat can be a central part of the purchase decision.</p>
<h2>How to slice them</h2>
<p>A slight diagonal cut works well for narrow fuet because it creates a broader slice. Large salchichón should generally be cut at a moderate thickness so neither chewiness nor fragility dominates.</p>
<h2>Storage after opening</h2>
<p>Both are cured products, but once cut they are more exposed to oxygen and drying. Protect the cut surface and follow the producer's storage instructions. For pre-sliced packs, follow the label after opening.</p>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Fuet and salchichón share a basic curing technique but represent different styles. Fuet is usually slim, quick-drying and delicately seasoned. Salchichón covers a broader range of formats and Iberian versions can provide especially distinctive fat and flavour.</p>
